0 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511420 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511420 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186510879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511420 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c7504c88-811b-566a-be94-9e43fa618465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511476 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511476 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(u-2v)\\cdot (v+2w).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cc2023e-80ab-5c70-bfaa-8494aaa05a23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511476 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2v)\\cdot (v+2w) & =u\\cdot v-2(v\\cdot v)+2(u\\cdot w)-4(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0-2(v\\cdot v)+2(u\\cdot w)-4(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =-2(3\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & +2((-2)\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 0+5\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & -4(3\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =-2(21)+2(-5)-4(-2) \\\\  & =-42-10+8 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511479 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511479 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511479 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511479 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -1 \\\\ b \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "308fc3e8-c583-59b4-999d-1160fdbc5784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511479 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4+b\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 2a-4-2b+10 & =0 \\\\ 2a-2b & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+b\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ a+3+b-6 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-2b=-6 \\\\ a+b=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-4b=-12\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-2(3) & =-6 \\\\ 2a-6 & =-6 \\\\ 2a & =0 \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=0-3=-3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511540 #edb/ref 1071 "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a905591c-e962-54f6-a8cc-c6c8bf83d953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511540 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $u⊥v$, they are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-5)+(-3)\\cdot 1+2k\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot k \\\\  & =-5-3+8k-2k \\\\  & =6k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k-8 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =8 \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511585 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ k \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -k-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that  $u⊥v.$ Find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fff3565b-721f-5145-afe3-b8fe4b216b1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511585 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 3+k\\cdot (-k-1)+k\\cdot (-7)+3\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =21-{k}^{2}-k-7k-36 \\\\  & =-{k}^{2}-8k-15 \\\\  & =-({k}^{2}+8k+15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({k}^{2}+8k+15) & =0 \\\\ -(k+5)(k+3) & =0 \\\\ k & =-5,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511601 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186511636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27b93922-d5e3-56f6-9165-297356e94309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186511540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511619 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d158392-5bc2-5a1a-99d8-29a76cb0bb41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218651